-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I22: the interface's method arrives under a MEMBER ALIAS.
--
-- 🚨 THIS ROW PINS A KNOWN-WRONG ANSWER. It is not a claim that 7 is correct. Under
-- S1-NS (a)(ii) the VALUE arm admits a method when the import path admits `n` "as a
-- callable method" -- and the only name this module can write for that method is `sz`.
-- It cannot write `size` for it at all. So `size` here unambiguously denotes the
-- standalone imported from `fmod`, and the correct answer is 99. It prints 7.
--
-- MECHANISM: `selectIfaceRows` narrows by the member ORIGIN spelling (`map fst bs`), so
-- `{size as sz}` admits `size` -- the name in the DEPENDENCY -- rather than `sz`, the name
-- this module can call.
--
-- 🚨 THE FIX SHAPE IS NOT KNOWN, AND THE OBVIOUS ONE IS INERT. This header used to end
-- "the fix is one token: key the VALUE arm on `snd` while the TYPE arm keeps `fst`". That
-- change cannot move this file's answer, and it is DERIVABLE without building anything:
--
--     grep -n 'renameAliasedMethods \|graphIfaceMethods coreDecls' compiler/types/typecheck.mdk
--
-- `renameAliasedMethods` has exactly one call site, in `elaborateModules`, and it runs
-- BEFORE that driver writes `graphIfaceMethodsRef` and before the mark pass. It applies
-- `deAliasMethodImports`, which rewrites `import ifc.{size as sz}` into `import ifc.{size}`
-- -- so by the time `selectIfaceRows` runs on the run/build path, `snd b == fst b` for every
-- member and the two keyings are the same expression. A reviewer implemented the one-token
-- change, rebuilt, and measured this file still at 7, which is what the derivation predicts.
-- (The `check` path does not de-alias, so re-keying would move that arm alone -- a
-- `check`/`run` split, not a fix.) A real fix spans at least those two functions and is a
-- design question: not de-aliasing breaks aliased method CALLS outright, which is why
-- `deAliasMethodImports` exists at all. A DOCUMENTED FIX SHAPE THAT IS INERT IS WORSE THAN
-- NONE -- the next person implements it, sees nothing move, and looks for the defect
-- somewhere else. The PINNED VALUES below are correct and this cell is verified
-- fail-capable: under a genuine fix main.mdk moves 7 -> 99 while bare.mdk stays 99.
--
-- ⚠️ NOT A REGRESSION, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS PINNED RATHER THAN FIXED HERE. The base
-- compiler prints 7 too -- the member-alias arm is exactly where this unit changes nothing.
-- Re-keying it is a behaviour change on an axis no ruling covers, and this cell exists so
-- that change is graded when someone makes it rather than discovered afterwards.
--
-- The DISCRIMINATOR is the second import line, and `bare.mdk` beside this file is the same
-- program with `import ifc.{size as sz}` replaced by a bare `import ifc`: that one prints
-- 99, correctly, because a bare import binds no names at all. Two spellings that admit the
-- same amount of the interface -- nothing writable -- and only one of them answers right.
--
-- An earlier source comment claimed this deviation was "unobservable today because
-- member-aliased method imports are independently broken". Both halves are false: `sz "ab"`
-- prints 7, so they work; and this pair reaches the difference.
import fmod.{size}
import ifc.{size as sz}

main = println (size "abc")
